{"name":"Red Pepper","hex":"#7a3f38","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"red pepper","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A red-colored, ripe, bell or sweet pepper. Unripe peppers of this variety are green and edible but not as sweet.","Spicy pepper."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red_pepper"}],"entries":[{"title":"Red Pepper (musical)","blocks":[{"text":"Red Pepper is a musical in two acts with music by Albert Gumble and Owen Murphy, lyrics by Howard Emmett Rogers, and a book by Edgar Smith and Emily Young. Staged on Broadway in 1922 after a premiere in Baltimore in 1921, the musical was created for the comedy duo of McIntyre and Heath, who were famous for their performances in blackface."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Senora_from_Red_Pepper.jpg/330px-Senora_from_Red_Pepper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_Pepper_(musical)","wikidata":"Q125398750"},{"title":"Red Peppers","blocks":[{"text":"Red Peppers, described as \"an interlude with music\", is a short comic play in two scenes by Noël Coward. It is one of ten short plays that make up Tonight at 8.30, a cycle written to be performed in groups of three plays across three evenings. The original production, starring Coward and Gertrude Lawrence played in a pre-London tour, and then the West End, and finally New York, in 1935–1937. Red Peppers has been revived periodically and has been adapted for the cinema and television."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/bf/Red_Peppers_cast.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_Peppers","wikidata":"Q7304718"},{"title":"Red Pepper (newspaper)","blocks":[{"text":"Red Pepper is a daily tabloid newspaper in Uganda that began publication on 19 June 2001. Mirroring tabloid styles in other countries, the paper is known for its mix of sensationalism, scandal, and frequent nudity. The paper has faced criticism from the Ugandan government over its publication of content described as conspiracy theories relating to the death of Sudan's Vice President John Garang in a helicopter crash and revealing that former foreign minister James Wapakhabulo died of AIDS."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_Pepper_(newspaper)","wikidata":"Q7304716"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg/330px-Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red","wikidata":"Q3142"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/McIntyre_and_Heath.jpg/500px-McIntyre_and_Heath.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/McIntyre_and_Heath.jpg/1280px-McIntyre_and_Heath.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/McIntyre_and_Heath.jpg/500px-McIntyre_and_Heath.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Photograph of McIntyre and Heath from 1908","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMcIntyre_and_Heath.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8